Subject: [PATCH 08/21] core: Fire Emblem Fates' randomizer wired: mode pick,
 saves, states, placement files

The Fates port's owner asked (its docs/DECISIONS.md F-044, engine build
OK 2026-11-20) for the randomizer settings inside the game, right after
picking Casual or Classic. Patch 0007 added the writer; this wires it.
Nothing changes for any other game, or for Fates without a randomized
run (every hook returns at once).

- New module fates_rando_session: the Session System now carries as
  system.rando. System::run_frame calls after_frame after end_frame, so
  window, headless and boot-server runs share it. It only reads memory
  (NewGameWatch) until the mode pick.
- With a window the frontend sets system.rando.interactive and answers
  the pick from its page (take_pending, answer). Otherwise the answer is
  taken at once: a queued one (boot-server rando-answer FILE), else the
  file FEF_RANDO_ANSWER names, else Randomizer off.
- answer() runs the port's tools/randomizer/rando.py make --patch-out
  hidden (CREATE_NO_WINDOW, stdin null, output piped into the log,
  killed after 120 s), checks every cell against GameData in memory and
  lays all or none. The run is kept in
  <data>/rando/0004000000179800/runs/<seed>-<8hex>/.
- Saves: every open of a slot file for reading at the title lays that
  save's run or puts the original tables back (side file
  slots/<save>.json, written when a slot is written). Save states:
  state_run, state_restore, and the <state>.rando.json sidecar.
- Placement files: new module fates_rando_file (LZ13/11, the IS .bin
  form, optimal LZ11 recompress). A read of romfs:/ that covers a
  placement file of the active run gets a patched copy when it fits the
  stored size; when it does not, the cells are checked and laid in
  memory at the first system call after the game stores the file
  (kernel::svc::dispatch calls before_svc, a no-op otherwise).

Our own code and words; no game data. Unit tests use fake memory and,
when the port's files are given by environment, its extracted RomFS.
